        "title": "Dyeing and Fiber Arts - Landscape and Taxonomy",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nFIBER TAXONOMY\n  |\n  +-- PROTEIN FIBERS (animal, amide bonds, -CO-NH-)\n  |   +-- HAIR FIBERS\n  |   |   Wool (Ovis aries): dominant, keratinous, scales\n  |   |   Cashmere (Capra hircus): fine underfleece\n  |   |   Alpaca (Vicugna pacos): no scales = less felting\n  |   |   Angora (rabbit): very fine, hollow\n  |   |   Mohair (Angora goat): lustrous, minimal scales\n  |   +-- FILAMENT\n  |       Silk (Bombyx mori): continuous filament, fibroin + sericin\n  |       Wild silk (Tussah): coarser, yellow\n  |\n  +-- CELLULOSE FIBERS (plant, beta-1,4-glycosidic bonds, -OH groups)\n      +-- SEED HAIR\n      |   Cotton (Gossypium): short staple, seeds\n      |   Kapok (Ceiba): non-spinnable hollow fibers\n      +-- BAST (stem fibers)\n      |   Flax (Linum usitatissimum) -> linen: strong, lustrous\n      |   Hemp (Cannabis sativa): coarser, sustainable\n      |   Jute (Corchorus): coarse, bags and hessian\n      |   Ramie (Boehmeria): very strong, rigid\n      +-- LEAF\n          Sisal (Agave sisalana): rope\n```"
    },
