        "title": "Dyeing and Fiber Arts - Landscape and Taxonomy",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Why fiber type matters for dyeing**: Protein fibers (wool, silk) have abundant amine groups (-NH2) that coordinate metal mordants. Acid dyes bond ionically to the protonated amine groups. Cellulose fibers (cotton, linen) lack these groups — they need reactive dyes (covalent bonds), vat dyes (physical entrapment), or tannin pre-mordanting before alum mordanting."
    }
